\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\looseness=-1
The confusability signal applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). Modest pairwise $r^2$ amplifies ${\sim}$3$\times$ at batch level, corroborated by IntraSub--BCS monotonicity (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}). The NER--RE asymmetry reflects dispersed error modes and compressed BCS variance (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ner_power}). We hypothesize that SCS's predictive power rests on partial alignment between embedding spaces and LLM type representations: near-neighbor types activate overlapping representations, producing interference---consistent with ecological confusability \citep{baan-2023-ecological}. This operates on type substitution specifically, explaining the RE--NER asymmetry; the remaining ${\sim}$73\% of variance likely reflects prompt formatting, definition quality, and mention ambiguity.

\looseness=-1
For NER, reducing candidate types is the primary lever; for RE, pre-computing SCS/BCS flags high-risk schemas---a principle that may extend to other schema-grounded tasks (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-domain}). Naive mitigations (renaming, few-shot) do not close the gap (Appendices~\ref{app:renaming},~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}). Decompose-then-merge fails (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}), but oracle routing recovers accuracy. Separability holds on the linear scale but remains open on the logit scale.

