\begin{abstract}
Schema complexity affects training-free LLM information extraction (IE), yet prior work conflates two factors: having more types to discriminate among (count) and having semantically similar types to confuse (confusability). We present, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors in zero-shot LLM IE across four datasets, focusing on relation extraction (RE) as the primary testbed with named entity recognition (NER) and event detection as boundary conditions.
Using embedding-based confusability metrics with controlled batch design, we find:
(1)~count degradation spans NER and RE (up to $-$49\,pp), and inter-type confusability independently predicts RE type substitution errors, with bounded interaction confirmed by equivalence testing;
(2)~batch confusability independently explains ${\sim}$27\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance on FewRel (semipartial $R^2 = 0.273$), replicating across two model families at three scales (8B--70B), while the NER null serves as a boundary condition;
(3)~schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification and routing failure, though oracle routing recovers accuracy.
\end{abstract}

